            <ArticleTitle>Association of a COL1A1 polymorphism with lumbar disc disease in young military recruits.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e44</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Lumbar disc disease (LDD), one of the most common conditions for which patients seek medical care, has been associated with sequence changes of the COL genes. COL1A1, however, has not been studied in young patients with LDD; COL1A1 polymorphisms have been associated with bone mineral density (BMD) in several populations and with LDD in older adults.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To study COL1A1 polymorphisms in young Greek army recruits with LDD.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">These young soldiers were diagnosed with early LDD at the time of their presentation to a military training site. All patients had radiological confirmation of their disease; a control group was also studied.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sp1-binding site polymorphism of the COL1A1 gene was investigated by standard methods.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was an increased frequency of the &quot;ss&quot; genotype (33.3%) in LDD patients; none of the controls had this genotype. In addition, a significantly smaller number of controls was heterozygotes for this allele.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A previously studied sequence change of the regulatory region of the COL1A1 gene, the same as has previously been associated with low BMD in many populations and LDD in older adults, showed a strong association with LDD in young male soldiers who were recently diagnosed with this disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
